# probe_pair_bound_name_takes_a_value.gy — where the pair still has nowhere to go.
#
# A name the arithmetic door bound carries its kind in the tag beside its payload (ADR 0267), and the
# positions that ask for one number read it (Gap R.143, programs/probe_pair_bound_name_as_a_number). What is
# left is the positions that take a whole *value*: a builtin's argument keeps one word for its operand, and
# the second word — the tag — has no place to be stored, so the compiled leg declines rather than print the
# payload alone. CPython answers both lines (roadmap Gap R.146, the same missing word Gap R.139 names on the
# calling side).
#
# Two lines left this program as the shape they were filed with changed sides. An operator that chooses an
# operand can ask the pair directly, so `n and 3` prints CPython's `3` (Gap R.147, ADR 0269); and a container
# element is no longer a one-word position — `[n]` prints `[14]`, the heap builder writing the element's tag
# beside its payload — so that line lives in programs/probe_pair_bound_name_enters_a_container.gy (Gap R.146's
# element half, ADR 0306). A dict entry, a set member and a literal `sum`/`min`/`max` folds into a static
# array still owe the same missing word.
xs = []
xs.append([7, 8])
n = xs[0][0] * 2
print(abs(n))
print(min(n, 3))
print(n and 3)
